WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.040
Iceman: Ace fighter pilot,&nbsp;
volleyball enthusiast… and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.180
the real hero of the Top Gun franchise?&nbsp;
It might not be as crazy as it sounds.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.000
Top Gun was penned by the screenwriting&nbsp;
team of Jack Epps Jr. and Jim Cash, who,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.500
in the 1980s, were two of the hottest&nbsp;
writers in the business. As they worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:23.460
on the Top Gun script, they realized on one&nbsp;
level that it's actually more of a sports&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:29.280
film than a war movie — which opened up many new&nbsp;
possibilities for their story. As Epps explains:

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.040
“From my point of view as a writer,&nbsp;
'Top Gun' is a sport is a sport movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.980
and the sport is ACM: Aerial Combat Maneuvering."

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:39.300
Much like in the world of sports, Top&nbsp;
Gun features a lot of young talent who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:44.040
are eager to prove themselves. To that end,&nbsp;
the writers set a number of important scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:48.000
in locker rooms — mostly because this is&nbsp;
a place where the characters can speak to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.180
each other in a manner that might not&nbsp;
be possible when they're in uniform.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.040
It's not clear whether the characters in&nbsp;
Top Gun are based on real people or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:01.200
but some people believe that Iceman was indeed&nbsp;
based on a real-life fighter pilot. According to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.600
the military publication Stars and Stripes, Ice&nbsp;
was based on a fighter pilot named Deniz Tek,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:11.700
who went by the same callsign. Tek played&nbsp;
guitar in a cult band called Radio Birdman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:16.860
and studied medicine in Australia. Once&nbsp;
he got back to the United States in 1981,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.680
he joined the Navy and became a flight surgeon.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.060
According to Tek himself, the makers of&nbsp;
Top Gun spent time with his squadron doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:27.720
research for the film. In fact, one&nbsp;
of Tek's old military buddies said:

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:32.460
"The characters seem so similar. He was&nbsp;
even more intense than the movie Iceman."

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:36.720
Yet Jack Epps Jr., who co-wrote the&nbsp;
film, said the character wasn't based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:40.560
on any real-life military man, insisting&nbsp;
that basing their characters on real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.160
people might have brought up unwanted&nbsp;
legal problems for the film's crew.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:48.960
In the movie Sleep With Me, Quentin&nbsp;
Tarantino delivered a riff on Top Gun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.760
claiming that it's actually a gay movie in&nbsp;
disguise. Since then, numerous theories have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:59.340
popped up online about the apparent homoerotic&nbsp;
subtext of Top Gun. But David Paul Kirkpatrick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.020
a former executive at Paramount, says this&nbsp;
wasn't intentional. He wrote on Facebook:

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:07.200
"Much has been made of the [...]&nbsp;
homo-erotic nature of Top Gun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:12.180
particularly the volleyball sequence,&nbsp;
but it wasn't intentional. The guys in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.760
the movie were young and in good&nbsp;
shape for their respective parts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:19.380
and Tony Scott shot the scene quickly at&nbsp;
magic hour which gave it that golden glow."

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:21.060
Kirkpatrick continued:

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:25.620
"The volleyball sequence stayed in the movie only&nbsp;
because the women adored the scene in previews.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:31.200
It's because the sequence exhibited non-toxic&nbsp;
masculinity at its finest — non-aggressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:34.920
Greek gods playing ball slathered in&nbsp;
suntan lotion and wearing Ray Bans."

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:39.540
In later years, however, the film attracted&nbsp;
a significant gay following, and it brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:44.880
many in touch with their sexuality at a young&nbsp;
age. As Robert W. Fieseler explained in Slate:

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.800
"Iceman and Maverick jetted lifesaving&nbsp;
homoeroticism past a generation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.240
conservative parents [...] No adult, not&nbsp;
even a minister, would bat an eyelash when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:59.640
I said knowingly to their sons—kids I hoped were&nbsp;
closeted gay, too—'Top Gun is my favorite film.'"

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:05.280
It actually took some convincing to get Val&nbsp;
Kilmer to commit to Top Gun. As Kilmer recalled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:10.320
he and Cruise had the same agent, so he knew&nbsp;
about the project. But he wasn't convinced.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:17.640
"I just really wasn't interested in it. I liked&nbsp;
Tony Scott very much, but I turned it down."

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:21.480
As Kilmer remembered, director&nbsp;
Tony Scott then ran after Kilmer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:24.600
got in front of the elevator so&nbsp;
he couldn't leave, and told him:

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:28.740
"You've gotta do it man! It's gonna be great! And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:33.780
he described with this enthusiasm and&nbsp;
relish how it was going to be seen."

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:37.680
Kilmer was finally won over&nbsp;
by his enthusiasm — although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:41.160
a number of contractual obligations&nbsp;
to Paramount helped things along.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:46.200
In a movie full of cocky young guys out to&nbsp;
prove themselves, it would only be natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:50.160
there would be some tension offscreen&nbsp;
as well. Whether there was indeed any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:55.140
true rivalry between Tom Cruise and Val Kilmer is&nbsp;
unclear, but they certainly kept their distance.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:59.580
Often, when actors play rivals in a film,&nbsp;
they'll stay away from each other while the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:04.620
movie is shooting. On Top Gun, Kilmer stayed close&nbsp;
with his onscreen buddies Hollywood and Wolfman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:08.760
while Maverick and Goose stayed tight.&nbsp;
Cruise also stayed out of any of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:13.440
offscreen partying that Kilmer and his Top Gun&nbsp;
buddies got involved in. Kilmer later said:

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:17.640
"I would purposely play up the rivalry&nbsp;
between Tom's character and mine off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:20.940
screen as well. It was fun to play up&nbsp;
the conflict between our characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:25.800
but in reality, I've always thought of Tom as a&nbsp;
friend, and we've always supported each other."

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:30.540
Not all was well on the Top Gun set, though.&nbsp;
In a documentary on the making of the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:34.080
Kilmer said he believed Cruise might&nbsp;
have sabotaged the footage from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:38.340
volleyball game. Tony Scott apparently&nbsp;
told Kilmer the shots featuring him had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:43.260
been "cooked," meaning they were over or&nbsp;
under-developed, and unusable for the film.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:47.700
In order to deliver their best&nbsp;
performances, many actors craft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:51.300
backstories for the characters they play.&nbsp;
The audience may never know about this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:54.480
but it's nevertheless important for&nbsp;
actors to have something to draw on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:59.040
when they perform. When Kilmer agreed to&nbsp;
play Iceman, he created a backstory for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:03.780
the character that gave him some added depth.&nbsp;
As the actor said in his documentary, Val:

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:08.100
"On the page, there was very little to&nbsp;
the character Iceman. So I attempted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.600
make him real. I manifested a backstory for&nbsp;
him, where he had a father who ignored him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:16.500
and as a result, was driven by the&nbsp;
need to be perfect in every way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:19.920
This obsession with perfections&nbsp;
is what made him so arrogant."

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:24.720
This backstory brings so much more to Top Gun&nbsp;
because Maverick struggles with similar issues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:28.560
as he desperately seeks to live up to&nbsp;
his late father's legend as a pilot.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:35.160
Top Gun i s a story that inspired audiences around&nbsp;
the world. It was the number one movie of 1986,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:40.860
making $176 million at the domestic&nbsp;
box office. Since starring in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:45.420
Kilmer has been recognized as Iceman&nbsp;
for over three decades by pilots — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:47.880
the film famously increased military enlistment.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:52.920
Reportedly, the U.S. Navy had more applications&nbsp;
than they'd had in years after the success of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:57.720
Top Gun, and there have been reports that the&nbsp;
film made naval recruiting increase by 500%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:03.000
though this figure may have been exaggerated.&nbsp;
At the same time, as one Navy vet said:

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.740
"I saw the movie, thought it looked like the&nbsp;
most exciting job in the world. And it is."

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:12.360
In recent years, the Navy has been having&nbsp;
problems enlisting new recruits; it remains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:15.540
to be seen whether Top Gun: Maverick&nbsp;
will turn this around for the service.

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:21.420
Tom Cruise was clearly the star of Top Gun, and&nbsp;
he starred in the film right as he was becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:25.500
one of the hottest actors in Hollywood. In&nbsp;
recent years, however, some fans have come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:30.120
to realize how important Iceman is to the&nbsp;
story, with many even believing that he is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:34.860
the real hero of the movie. In one critique&nbsp;
at Collider, for example, the writer noted:

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:37.920
"Iceman is just as essential&nbsp;
to the success of the final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:40.260
mission where Maverick gets all [his] acclaim."

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:44.940
In the end, Maverick gets all the glory,&nbsp;
but Maverick is more traditionally heroic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:51.000
both as a pilot and as a character. As many other&nbsp;
fans point out, Maverick himself is reckless and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:56.100
dangerous, but Iceman is cool under pressure and&nbsp;
far more together than the young protagonist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.100 --> 00:07:00.480
who strives to prove he's a real hotshot.&nbsp;
And whether you think he's the hero or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:06.300
there's no question Iceman is a crucial component&nbsp;
in the Top Gun storyline — after all, without him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:10.920
Maverick's character arc and growth wouldn't&nbsp;
be anywhere near as dramatic or compelling.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:14.880
"Who's the better pilot, you or me?"

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:20.730
"This is a nice moment. Let's not ruin it."

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:23.040
Although Tom Cruise is the&nbsp;
star of Top Gun: Maverick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:27.600
the fact that Iceman returned for a brief but&nbsp;
memorable segment shows there is still plenty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:31.980
of goodwill for the character. In fact,&nbsp;
as producer Jerry Bruckheimer explained:

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:34.740
"Tom said he wasn't going to&nbsp;
make the movie without him."

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:37.500
Cruise confirmed this, too. As he recalls:

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:41.400
"I really rallied hard for him to make&nbsp;
the movie. The kind of talent that he has,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.620
and you see that scene, it's very special."

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:47.340
Cruise apparently told Bruckheimer&nbsp;
that Kilmer simply had to come back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:51.660
for the second Top Gun movie. It was a&nbsp;
wonderful reunion for Bruckheimer, too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:54.360
who had worked with Kilmer&nbsp;
on the original Top Gun.

00:07:55.380 --> 00:08:00.120
While Val Kilmer was initially skittish about&nbsp;
taking on the role of Iceman in the original Top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:04.560
Gun, he was reportedly delighted that his&nbsp;
character is still so beloved by fans and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:09.300
filmmakers. That's exactly why he signed on to&nbsp;
one of the biggest box office hits of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:13.320
with Top Gun: Maverick. In fact,&nbsp;
as Jerry Bruckheimer explained:

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:17.820
"We called Val and told him we wanted to meet&nbsp;
him to be in the picture. And he was thrilled."

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:20.460
And as Kilmer's son Jack told People magazine:

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:24.120
"They honor the legacy of Iceman,&nbsp;
and he was so stoked by it."

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:28.200
When Jack and his sister Mercedes went&nbsp;
down to the San Diego naval base where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:32.100
Maverick was shooting, real-life navy&nbsp;
pilots came up to them and told them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:35.820
that Top Gun had inspired them to&nbsp;
join the armed forces. Jack said:

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:39.600
"It was one of the proudest moments of being&nbsp;
American that you could think of really."

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:44.160
Against all odds, especially with Kilmer's&nbsp;
speaking voice being so badly damaged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:48.540
Kilmer's scene with Cruise in Maverick makes&nbsp;
for one of the movie's most powerful moments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:52.200
Kilmer later said that, when Iceman and&nbsp;
Maverick hug in their scene together:

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:54.960
"[It was] straight from the heart. I love Tom."

00:08:54.960 --> 00:09:00.720
On Top Gun: Maverick, Val Kilmer had a hand&nbsp;
in developing Iceman's story. In the sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:05.580
Iceman has moved up to admiral, and his life is&nbsp;
coming to an end due to a devastating illness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:10.560
It was actually Kilmer's idea to have Iceman&nbsp;
be a mentor to Maverick, and he discussed his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:16.380
ideas for the role with Cruise, Maverick director&nbsp;
Joseph Kosinski, and Jerry Bruckheimer. At first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:20.760
Kilmer types out his dialog to Cruise,&nbsp;
then he finally speaks, telling him:

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:24.840
"The Navy needs Maverick."

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:28.860
Watching the finished segment of Maverick&nbsp;
and Iceman together, Kilmer recalled:

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:34.740
"I was very moved the first time I saw it.&nbsp;
Almost 40 years is a long time for a reunion."

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:40.140
The door was always open for Iceman to return&nbsp;
in the second Top Gun film. The problem was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:45.060
that Val Kilmer had survived a diagnosis of throat&nbsp;
cancer and now has trouble speaking as a result.

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:50.280
But with the help of today's technology,&nbsp;
Kilmer was able to return to Top Gun. Kilmer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:54.240
worked with a company called Sonantic, who&nbsp;
created an A.I. voice that was put together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:58.920
from hours of footage of Kilmer speaking in&nbsp;
movies. These recordings were then run into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:03.720
algorithms to create a new voice for the&nbsp;
actor. As an executive at Sonantic said:

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:06.300
"In the end, we generated more than 40 different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:10.380
voice models and selected the best,&nbsp;
highest-quality, most expressive one."

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:15.300
In the future, other actors will be able to&nbsp;
recreate their voices like this as well. Kilmer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:19.260
said in a statement that he was grateful that&nbsp;
the company could restore his voice, and that:

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:23.220
"The chance to narrate my story, in a&nbsp;
voice that feels authentic and familiar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:25.320
is an incredibly special gift."

